There are 22 scrolls all together (including blank scrolls which I count
pre-identified, excluding mail).
There are 4 scrolls that may easily ruin your game independent of its c/u/b
status, especially for a newbie.
There are an additional 6 (or 7/8) scrolls that I consider to have negative
effects if the scrolls are cursed (not counting if it gets dark or so).
There are 4 (or 5) scrolls with real positive effects, if the scrolls are
non-cursed.
The majority of scrolls is just wasted if read to identify them.
The above values should just give a clue about the statistical distribution.

If you determine the c/u/b status of the scrolls, and take precautions (as
suggested), and chose the appropriate time of application, then you might
reduce the risk, or increase some gain.  Even if there is no identify in
your inventory or in the supply of a shop, you should note the price of
each scroll before you read them so that you are informed for the next time.
But the "just read" approach is very good to ruin a newbie game, because to
determine an appropriate time of application and appropriate pre-cautions
would likely be impossible.  It's russian roulette.

OTOH, if you have the luck to early "identify-by-read" all the bad scrolls
and *still* survive, you may make it easier to the middle game - but not
to the endgame, IMO.

For a better approach have a look at "The Saga of Ellora the Elven Archer".

